A1. No matter how the game is played, this one gets the grand slams. ~ GOVERNMENT
A11. Here is where you get a mixture of spicy things with plenty of French dressing. ~ PARIS
A12. The kind of person who is anxious to win. ~ EAGER
A13. An objective we should have when declining. ~ US
A15. These are the cause of the current uprising. ~ FLUES
A16. Oodles of. ~ GALORE
A17. The only way to get these is on the installment plan. ~ SERIALS
A19. The rich are liable to be up to their necks in this during the winter. ~ FUR
A20. Briefly speaking this is all I have. ~ IVE
A21. What Miss O'Uri calls her Louis, and Minnie Sota calls her Paul. ~ SAINT
A22. Twice there has been an unusual spell of this in Cincinnati. ~ SIN
A23. What a plumber often does with his tools. ~ USES
A24. Last year this was getting "nothing for something." ~ INVESTING
A27. Because this happened to be right, it made a big hit with Schmeling. ~ ARM
A28. Dealers are now giving one of these with each suit. ~ ACES
A29. A cockney might tell you there was nothing to this. ~ OLE
A30. An 'andy wife to 'ave around the 'ouse. ~ MIN
A31. The last sound of horn. ~ RN
A33. Sometimes this is put in for a catch, but if you are clever you can see right through it. ~ NET
A34. What the film star got into when he took up the role of a Spaniard. (pl.) ~ BOLEROS
A36. This is always hanging around American tourists in Hawaii. ~ LEI
A37. A representative of an American liner. ~ SS
A38. Early to bed and early to rise, the rule of this fellow of enormous size. ~ SOL
A39. Where the Cubs are convalescing. ~ ZOO
A40. This should be taken in hand before a cut is made. ~ HAFT
A41. A place for change and rest. (The bell-hops get the change and the manager gets the rest.) ~ SPA
A42. Have you seen this stand on the corner? When you look at it you will find it lies part of the time. ~ NEWSPAPER
A44. A species of cat that is apish. ~ COPY
A46. This was carried by a mason to the highest court. ~ HOD
A47. When the Barrymores were young, they used to play in this old-fashioned house. ~ OPERA
A48. A human thing to do. (But can you convince the other fellow of this?) ~ ERR
A49. One of two things you can get for a quarter. ~ BIT
A50. One of the three Moran sisters. ~ TRIPLET
A52. His first ante in the gambling game. (plur.) ~ AGATES
A54. Grandmothers are never sick on this kind of day. ~ RAINY
A55. The ease one gets from freedom. ~ EE
A56. The way one feels the morning after. ~ SANER
A57. The Frenchman who produced "The Danse of the Nymphs." ~ COROT
A58. What a ball-player has to make during the season in order to be a player the next year. ~ ENGAGEMENT

D1. All of this was divided into three parts. ~ GAUL
D2. Metal containers. ~ ORES
D3. This showed the strength of the Latins. ~ VIS
D4. Add this to your loss and see how it increases. ~ ES
D5. What the wets think their beer is now. ~ NEAR
D6. A bad beginning and a dismal ending. ~ MAL
D7. Looking at the world through I glasses. ~ EGOISM
D8. It is unlucky to lose this on one's wedding day. ~ NERVE
D9. Big fruit growers from California. ~ TREES
D10. Nicholas N. Slotsky, Ivan Awfulitch and Ignoritz Vodka. ~ RUSSIANS
D11. These represent lots and lots. ~ PLATS
D14. Helps to keep one's spirits up. (plur.) ~ SEANCES
D15. A ten dollar word used in the Judge's sentence. (plur.) ~ FINES
D16. The boss fired this one without any notice. ~ GUN
D18. What he had when he made a bolt for the door. ~ RIVET
D19. She didn't care this for the man but she did like his dates. ~ FIG
D22. What someone did when Dr. Sze was pronounced minister from China. ~ SNEEZED
D23. An ash receiver. ~ URN
D25. A catspaw. ~ TOOL
D26. What Chicago is when her joints are stiff and her temperature reaches 102 degrees. ~ ILL
D27. Introducing Mr. Bradypus Tridactylus from South America. (You will remember him by his 3 toes.) (plur.) ~ AIS
D30. Stock quotations. ~ MOOS
D31. He causes lots of ire....ump ire. ~ REFEREE
D32. We can not tell you what these are but we do know the day rates are $3 and up. (Ask your chemist.) ~ NITRATES
D34. This has a tight clutch. ~ BOA
D35. A broil that many people have for breakfast. ~ ROW
D36. This helps to put up a good front. (in Dakota) ~ LAPEL
D38. It was executed in 1776. ~ SPY
D40. One of the Hooligan family. ~ HAPPY
D41. When spring is past. (This is a tense one.) ~ SPRANG
D42. This will deny everything. ~ NOT
D43. A blind man can see this when a dumb man can not. ~ POINT
D44. Wonders will never . . . . . . . They used to get down from ducks but now they find they can get down from an elephant. ~ CEASE
D45. This was given the air for sound reasons. ~ ORGAN
D46. What a single millionaire's income is. ~ HIS
D49. Justice and that is all. ~ BERG
D50. A mountain lake. ~ TARN
D51. You might find this at a bargain counter. ~ RIOT
D53. This is forever getting into hot water. ~ TEA
D54. A deer friend from Europe. ~ ROE
D57. The author signing off. (I L B C ing U.) ~ CM


